WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Many Go validation paths begin with string conversion.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:14.040
strconv.Atoi and strconv.ParseInt are common tools for converting string input into integers while allowing conversion failures to be handled explicitly.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:22.940
Parsing unsigned values, page numbers, limits, offsets, IDs, counts, and quantities should be followed by range checks.

4
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:30.400
A value can parse successfully and still be too small, too large, negative when it should not be, or outside a business rule.

5
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:39.400
Overflow and type size should be considered when input influences counts, indexes, allocation, pagination, or file offsets.

6
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:47.340
ParseInt accepts a bit size, but the result still needs to be checked before conversion into int, uint, or another narrower type.

7
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.600
A successful parse is not permission to use the number everywhere.

8
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:57.060
It only means the input could be interpreted as a number under the parsing rule you chose.

9
00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:08.220
Time parsing is security-relevant because expiration, scheduling, access windows, retention, audit timelines, and workflow state often depend on time values.

10
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.880
time.Parse should be used with explicit layouts, and applications should normalize time zones intentionally.

11
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:25.300
A timestamp may parse and still be too far in the past, too far in the future, outside an allowed window, or inconsistent with another field in the same request.

12
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.860
Identifiers also need more than syntax checks.

13
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:36.480
A UUID-shaped value may not exist, may belong to a different tenant, or may refer to an object the caller cannot access.

14
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:42.100
A numeric ID may parse but be outside an allowed range or point to a record in the wrong state.

15
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.600
Validation should check format first, then meaning in context.

16
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:52.700
That separation keeps parsing errors, authorization checks, and business rules understandable.
